(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-pulm-nosocomial-pneumonia-quiz"]={"title":"Nosocomial Pneumonia & Its Antibiotics Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Vancomycin is primarily used in nosocomial pneumonia to cover which pathogen?","choices":["Methicillin-resistant S. aureus","Gram-negative bacilli such as Pseudomonas","Atypicals such as Legionella","Fungi such as Aspergillus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin is primarily used for empiric coverage of methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, the most common gram-positive cause of nosocomial pneumonia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which drug is a limited-spectrum empiric choice for nosocomial pneumonia without multidrug-resistant risk factors?","choices":["Cefepime","Meropenem","Vancomycin","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Limited-spectrum coverage is ceftriaxone, or levofloxacin or moxifloxacin, or ampicillin-sulbactam, or ertapenem. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why should fluoroquinolones be used judiciously in nosocomial pneumonia?","choices":["Poor lung tissue penetration","Weak activity against atypicals","They need no renal adjustment","Rising gram-negative resistance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Increasing use of fluoroquinolones is leading to gram-negative resistance, so they should be used judiciously. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A proposed empiric regimen for nosocomial pneumonia with multidrug-resistant risk factors is cefepime, piperacillin-tazobactam and vancomycin. What makes it inappropriate?","choices":["It has no gram-positive coverage","It has no antipseudomonal drug in it","It pairs two beta-lactam antibiotics","It needs linezolid in place of vancomycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cefepime and piperacillin-tazobactam are both antipseudomonal beta-lactams; the partner must be an antipseudomonal fluoroquinolone or an aminoglycoside. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which gram-negative bacillus is the most common cause of nosocomial pneumonia?","choices":["Escherichia coli","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Acinetobacter species"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pseudomonas aeruginosa is the most common gram-negative bacillus, followed by Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Acinetobacter, Enterobacter and Serratia; methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is the most common gram-positive coccus. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A hospitalized patient carries several risks for pneumonia. Which of them is one that cannot be changed?","choices":["Hospitalization in winter","Histamine H2 blocker therapy","Reintubation","An intracranial pressure monitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Unmodifiable risk factors are age over 70, chronic lung disease, depressed consciousness, large-volume aspiration and hospitalization during the winter months. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which drawback is listed for linezolid?","choices":["No oral form","Poor lung penetration","Renal dose adjustment","High cost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Linezolid is expensive, though it has great methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage, oral and intravenous forms, good lung penetration and no renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is ceftazidime rarely used as the antipseudomonal cephalosporin for nosocomial pneumonia?","choices":["It lowers the seizure threshold","It lacks Pseudomonas activity","It needs no renal adjustment","Resistance develops rapidly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ceftazidime (Fortaz) is not recommended because of rapid development of resistance and is rarely used; cefepime is the choice. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A proposed empiric regimen for nosocomial pneumonia with multidrug-resistant risk factors is vancomycin, linezolid and tobramycin. What makes it inappropriate?","choices":["It lacks gram-positive coverage","It has only one gram-negative agent","It pairs two beta-lactam antibiotics","Tobramycin cannot cover Pseudomonas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vancomycin and linezolid both cover methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), so only tobramycin covers gram-negative organisms; two are needed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A patient with methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus pneumonia is ready to finish therapy at home by mouth. Which drug allows this?","choices":["Linezolid","Vancomycin","Tobramycin","Cefepime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Linezolid has great methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage and comes in intravenous and oral forms, with good lung penetration. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A patient admitted for a hip fracture develops a new pneumonia on hospital day 4; it was not present on admission. How is this pneumonia classified?","choices":["Community-acquired pneumonia","Ventilator-associated pneumonia","Hospital-acquired pneumonia","Acute bronchitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nosocomial pneumonia is a lung infection not present at admission and occurring more than 48 hours after admission; it includes hospital-acquired pneumonia. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which property of aminoglycosides is the reason for extended-interval (once daily or longer) dosing in pneumonia?","choices":["Excellent lung penetration","Minimal kidney toxicity","Poor lung penetration","Activity against atypicals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides penetrate the lung poorly, so bigger, less frequent doses give the higher concentrations needed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which drug's use is a modifiable risk factor for nosocomial pneumonia?","choices":["Famotidine","Albuterol","Montelukast","Cromolyn sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Histamine H2 blocker or antacid therapy is a modifiable risk factor for nosocomial pneumonia, and famotidine is a histamine H2 blocker. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which statement about meropenem, compared with imipenem-cilastatin, is correct?","choices":["It needs no renal adjustment","It carries less seizure risk","It has limited Pseudomonas activity","It is a fourth-generation cephalosporin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Meropenem (Merrem) carries less risk for seizures than imipenem-cilastatin, which is used with caution in patients with a seizure history. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient develops hospital-acquired pneumonia on hospital day 7 and has no other risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens. Which initial approach fits?","choices":["Limited-spectrum therapy","Broad-spectrum therapy","Await the sputum culture","Supportive care alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Late onset, 5 days or more, is enough on its own to call for broad-spectrum initial antibiotic therapy for multidrug-resistant pathogens. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which antipseudomonal agent is a penicillin combined with a beta-lactamase inhibitor?","choices":["Ampicillin-sulbactam","Imipenem-cilastatin","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Piperacillin-tazobactam"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Piperacillin-tazobactam (Zosyn) is the antipseudomonal penicillin with a beta-lactamase inhibitor, and it requires renal adjustment. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient fails therapy for nosocomial pneumonia and is found to have a pulmonary embolus. Which category of treatment failure is this?","choices":["Wrong diagnosis","Wrong therapy","A complication","Drug toxicity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A pulmonary embolus or a neoplasm is a wrong-diagnosis failure; resistant pathogens are wrong therapy, and Clostridioides difficile colitis, empyema or lung abscess are complications. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"When is a vancomycin trough level drawn?","choices":["Before the first dose","Before the third or fourth dose","Right after the first dose","At the end of the course"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The trough is drawn prior to the third to fourth dose, when vancomycin is at steady state. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What should happen to empiric nosocomial pneumonia therapy once cultures and sensitivities return?","choices":["Keep the full empiric regimen","Broaden it to cover fungi","Narrow it to the culture results","Add a second gram-positive drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Therapy is streamlined once cultures and sensitivities are available, scaling back antibiotic coverage as the cultures return. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which risk factor for nosocomial pneumonia is modifiable?","choices":["Chronic lung disease","Age over 70 years","A nasogastric tube","Depressed consciousness"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Modifiable risk factors are mechanical ventilation, a nasogastric tube, an intracranial pressure monitor, histamine H2 blocker or antacid therapy, previous antibiotics and reintubation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"For broad-spectrum empiric coverage of nosocomial pneumonia, which drugs supply the gram-positive coverage added to the gram-negative combination?","choices":["Ceftriaxone or ertapenem","Vancomycin or linezolid","Gentamicin or tobramycin","Azithromycin or doxycycline"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Broad coverage is gram-negative combination therapy plus gram-positive coverage with vancomycin or linezolid, for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
